<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!DOCTYPE plist PUBLIC "-//Apple//DTD PLIST 1.0//EN" "http://www.apple.com/DTDs/PropertyList-1.0.dtd">
<plist version="1.0">
<dict>
  <key>Label</key>
  <string>com.niche-hunter.backup</string>

  <!-- ***** NOT INSTALLED. The backup still runs from cron. *****

       This file is correct and ready, and installing it today would BREAK the
       backup. Measured 2026-08-30: bootstrapped and kickstarted, the agent could
       not even open its output file —

         backup_db.sh: line 48: .../niche_hunter_2026-08-30.db.gz:
         Operation not permitted        (agent exited 1)

       The destination is iCloud Drive (~/Library/Mobile Documents), which is
       TCC-protected. macOS grants Full Disk Access per RESPONSIBLE PROCESS: cron
       holds that grant on this machine and writes there every morning, and a
       launchd agent is a different responsible process holding nothing. So the
       agent fails harder than the missed-fire problem it was meant to solve —
       cron at least writes the backup.

       To finish the move: grant Full Disk Access to the agent in System Settings
       > Privacy & Security, then

         cp scripts/launchd/com.niche-hunter.backup.plist ~/Library/LaunchAgents/
         launchctl bootstrap gui/$UID ~/Library/LaunchAgents/com.niche-hunter.backup.plist
         launchctl kickstart -p gui/$UID/com.niche-hunter.backup

       and CHECK logs/backup.log says "backup ok" before removing the cron line.
       Until then the cron entry is the live one and must stay. -->

  <!-- As with the nightly agent: scripts/_common.sh owns the working directory,
       PATH and .env, so this file deliberately states neither. -->
  <key>ProgramArguments</key>
  <array>
    <string>/Users/mac/Projects/youtube/niche-hunter/scripts/backup_db.sh</string>
  </array>

  <!-- 30 minutes after the nightly starts. That is not a guarantee the nightly
       has FINISHED — a large run took 20 minutes on 2026-08-31 — but the backup
       uses sqlite3 `.backup`, which is safe against a live writer in WAL mode.
       That is the property the ordering rests on, not the gap.

       Here rather than in cron for the same reason as the nightly: a backup
       skipped because the machine was asleep is a day of the compounding asset
       with no offsite copy, and cron never retries it. -->
  <key>StartCalendarInterval</key>
  <dict>
    <key>Hour</key><integer>9</integer>
    <key>Minute</key><integer>40</integer>
  </dict>

  <key>StandardOutPath</key>
  <string>/Users/mac/Projects/youtube/niche-hunter/logs/backup.log</string>
  <key>StandardErrorPath</key>
  <string>/Users/mac/Projects/youtube/niche-hunter/logs/backup.log</string>

  <key>RunAtLoad</key>
  <false/>
</dict>
</plist>
